# The upstream relay credential (spec §11.18).
#
# ONE FILE FOR THE MACHINE, AND ROOT IS THE ONLY ACCOUNT THAT READS IT. That is
# the entire security argument for replacing msmtp with a local MTA. msmtp ran
# as the tenant, so its configuration had to be tenant-readable, so every
# customer on the box held the credential the server itself sends with — and
# one customer reading their own copy could send as the operator and get the
# machine blacklisted for everyone. Postfix's smtp(8) opens this map in its
# pre-jail initialisation, while it is still root, and only then drops to the
# unprivileged `postfix` user. Nothing else ever needs to open it, so nothing
# else is allowed to: 0600, root-owned.
#
# There is no compiled copy. `main.cf` names this map `texthash:`, which Postfix
# reads directly, so `postmap` is never run and the second file holding the same
# password — the one people chmod the source and forget — is never created.
#
# The key has to be spelled exactly as `relayhost` in main.cf, brackets and port
# included; both come from the same rendered value, so they cannot drift apart.
# A key that merely looks right is not a syntax error, it is an authentication
# that is silently never attempted.
{% if mail.username %}
{{ mail.relayhost }}	{{ mail.username }}:{{ mail.password }}
{% else %}
#
# This relay authorises by source IP and needs no credential, so the table is
# empty. The file still exists, and `main.cf` still does not reference it —
# `smtp_sasl_auth_enable` is off — because an absent map that something later
# starts pointing at is a fatal error at Postfix startup, and an empty one is
# not.
{% endif %}
